    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d21">

                <pb facs="0119" n="23" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg23"/>

                <head>No. 10.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Mr. Thomas O.
                    Osborn.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-06-20"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 20,
                        1879</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 109.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your interesting dispatch (No. 227) of May
                    3 last has been received and read with the attention bestowed on all matters
                    having relation <pb facs="0120" n="24" xml:id="pg24"/> to the unhappy struggle
                    which is convulsing the South American people, and which, I trust, a wise
                    statesmanship may at least restrain within its original bounds. It was hoped
                    that the boundary question between Chili and the Argentine Republic, to which
                    your dispatch more especially relates, was in a fair way of settlement.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c., &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
